#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "353e21f2-2d1d-55e3-ac86-19932d117997" :question/math-academy-id "e-21759" :question/problem "Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/ec87d268cc3be6e49308a705949867b5.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228880 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "28fd19b3-762a-572e-9b52-27e17093ad93" :question/math-academy-id "e-21762" :question/problem "Find an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/b144c85dbd922b70e678db80c285015e.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$.So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-10t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha$.\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n$0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}$\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\non the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition\n\n$$\nq(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(0) &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nAe^{-10(0)} + \\frac{3}{100} &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nA &= -\\frac{1}{100}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(t) &= -\\frac{1}{100}e^{-10t} + \\frac{3}{100} \\\\\n&= 1 100 (3 - e - 10 t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty$, the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228881 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e7a060-f36d-5fd6-a6f6-5b321261fb0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21763" :question/problem "Find the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/f517f979d06508ba19c194b407cfe616.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor with capacitance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t)\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$q_{p}|= α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t),; \\frac{dq_{p}}{dt}|=-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)) + 5(α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t))|= 3\\cos (6t); (-6α + 5β)\\sin (6t) + (5α + 6β)\\cos (6t)|= 3\\cos (6t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-6α + 5β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (6t)) \\\\ 5α + 6β = 3\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (6t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "e518865b-fc46-5d44-87dc-da0fa2424fde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228326}] :question/id #uuid "9547864c-5c79-5eab-ae35-a6351dc3262f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336594" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dq}{dt} + 6q = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
